1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,571
The eighth technique is comparative prompting.

2
00:00:02,571 --> 00:00:06,857
Use it when you want options, not a single answer.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:08,143
Two main shapes.

4
00:00:08,143 --> 00:00:09,429
First — compare.

5
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:13,286
"Compare Python and JavaScript for building a web scraper.

6
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:17,571
Create a table covering ease of learning, library support, performance,

7
00:00:17,571 --> 00:00:22,286
and best use cases." The AI gives you a side-by-side that

8
00:00:22,286 --> 00:00:23,571
helps you decide.

9
00:00:23,571 --> 00:00:24,857
Second — variations.

10
00:00:24,857 --> 00:00:27,429
"Write three versions of this headline.

11
00:00:27,429 --> 00:00:28,714
Professional and authoritative.

12
00:00:28,714 --> 00:00:30,000
Casual and friendly.

13
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,286
Urgent and action-oriented." You get three drafts in one round,

14
00:00:34,286 --> 00:00:36,857
you pick the one that lands.

15
00:00:36,857 --> 00:00:40,714
Comparative prompting is your tool for A/B testing copy,

16
00:00:40,714 --> 00:00:44,143
surfacing trade-offs in decisions, exploring before you commit.

17
00:00:44,143 --> 00:00:46,286
It's a productivity multiplier —

18
00:00:46,286 --> 00:00:49,286
one prompt does the work of three.

19
00:00:49,286 --> 00:00:51,857
And once you start using it,

20
00:00:51,857 --> 00:00:56,143
you'll wonder why you ever asked for a single version.

21
00:00:56,143 --> 00:00:57,857
Now four advanced patterns.

22
00:00:57,857 --> 00:01:00,857
These are concepts you'll absorb gradually —

23
00:01:00,857 --> 00:01:03,857
don't worry about memorizing them this pass.

24
00:01:03,857 --> 00:01:06,857
First — system prompts versus user prompts.

25
00:01:06,857 --> 00:01:09,000
If you're using an API,

26
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,714
or if your chat tool has a "custom instructions" or "personality"

27
00:01:13,714 --> 00:01:16,286
setting — that's a system prompt.

28
00:01:16,286 --> 00:01:17,486
It's persistent.

29
00:01:17,486 --> 00:01:20,914
It applies to every message in the conversation.

30
00:01:20,914 --> 00:01:24,771
The user prompt is what you type each time.

31
00:01:24,771 --> 00:01:27,771
Use the system prompt for stable behavior.

32
00:01:27,771 --> 00:01:31,200
"You are a coding assistant specialized in Python.

33
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,343
You write clear, well-commented code.

34
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:37,200
You always consider edge cases." That sets the room.

35
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,629
Then your user prompts handle the specific tasks.

36
00:01:40,629 --> 00:01:45,343
"Write a function to validate email addresses." "Now refactor it for

37
00:01:45,343 --> 00:01:50,057
readability." The analogy I like — system prompts are hiring instructions.

38
00:01:50,057 --> 00:01:52,200
User prompts are daily tasks.

39
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,200
You don't re-hire your assistant every morning.

40
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,200
You give them the job description once,

41
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,629
and after that you just hand them work.

42
00:02:01,629 --> 00:02:03,343
Pattern two — temperature.

43
00:02:03,343 --> 00:02:07,200
Temperature is a number, usually between zero and two,

44
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,629
that controls how random the AI's output is.

45
00:02:10,629 --> 00:02:15,343
Low temperature — say zero point two — gives you focused,

46
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:16,629
deterministic, consistent answers.

47
00:02:16,629 --> 00:02:20,057
Use it for code, data extraction, anything factual.

48
00:02:20,057 --> 00:02:23,057
Medium — around zero point seven —

49
00:02:23,057 --> 00:02:25,629
is the default for most tasks.

50
00:02:25,629 --> 00:02:26,829
Balanced.

51
00:02:26,829 --> 00:02:30,686
High — north of one — gives you variety.

52
00:02:30,686 --> 00:02:32,400
Brainstorming, creative writing, ideation.

53
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:36,257
Above one point two, output usually starts to degrade.

54
00:02:36,257 --> 00:02:37,543
So in practice,

55
00:02:37,543 --> 00:02:41,400
the useful range is zero to one point two.

56
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,543
Temperature isn't smarter or dumber.

57
00:02:43,543 --> 00:02:45,686
It's more or less random.

58
00:02:45,686 --> 00:02:46,886
Pick deliberately.

59
00:02:46,886 --> 00:02:51,600
There's a related parameter called top-p, sometimes called nucleus sampling —

60
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,314
most of the time you can leave it alone and adjust

61
00:02:56,314 --> 00:02:57,514
temperature only.

62
00:02:57,514 --> 00:02:59,657
Pattern three — prompt chaining.

63
00:02:59,657 --> 00:03:03,514
When a task is too complex for one prompt,

64
00:03:03,514 --> 00:03:05,229
break it into stages.

65
00:03:05,229 --> 00:03:09,086
Each stage uses the output of the previous one.

66
00:03:09,086 --> 00:03:13,371
For a research report — first prompt, generate the outline.

67
00:03:13,371 --> 00:03:18,086
Second, take each section of the outline and pull the facts.

68
00:03:18,086 --> 00:03:19,800
Third, write the prose.

69
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:21,514
Fourth, polish for flow.

70
00:03:21,514 --> 00:03:26,229
The benefit isn't just better output — though it is better.

71
00:03:26,229 --> 00:03:27,943
The benefit is debuggability.

72
00:03:27,943 --> 00:03:30,514
If the report turns out wrong,

73
00:03:30,514 --> 00:03:35,229
you can look at each stage independently and find the failure

74
00:03:35,229 --> 00:03:36,429
point.

75
00:03:36,429 --> 00:03:40,714
Try fixing one giant prompt, and you'll spend an hour.

76
00:03:40,714 --> 00:03:43,714
Find the broken stage in a chain,

77
00:03:43,714 --> 00:03:46,714
and you'll fix it in five minutes.

78
00:03:46,714 --> 00:03:48,857
Last pattern — meta prompting.

79
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:53,571
Meta prompting is when you ask the AI to help you

80
00:03:53,571 --> 00:03:55,286
write a better prompt.

81
00:03:55,286 --> 00:03:57,857
It sounds circular, but it works.

82
00:03:57,857 --> 00:04:02,143
"I want to generate creative product names for organic skincare.

83
00:04:02,143 --> 00:04:06,857
Help me write a prompt that consistently produces 2-3 word names,

84
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:08,571
nature-inspired, easy to pronounce.

85
00:04:08,571 --> 00:04:13,286
What prompt should I use?" The AI will write you a

86
00:04:13,286 --> 00:04:16,714
stronger prompt than you'd have written yourself —

87
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:21,429
because the AI has seen what good prompts in that domain

88
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:22,629
look like.

89
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:26,057
You're using it as a prompt engineering coach.

90
00:04:26,057 --> 00:04:29,057
Three places to reach for meta prompting.

91
00:04:29,057 --> 00:04:33,771
When you're new to prompt engineering and want a starting point.

92
00:04:33,771 --> 00:04:38,486
When your existing prompt isn't quite working and you can't tell

93
00:04:38,486 --> 00:04:39,686
why.

94
00:04:39,686 --> 00:04:44,400
And when you're tackling a domain you don't normally work in.

95
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:47,400
That's it for techniques and advanced patterns.

96
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:52,114
Open Workbook Task 4 — iterating on a creative brief —

97
00:04:52,114 --> 00:04:54,257
to put refinement into practice.

98
00:04:54,257 --> 00:04:58,543
In the next session, we'll cover the most common mistakes,

99
00:04:58,543 --> 00:05:01,543
plus three habits for safer prompting. ---
